    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/ai-reasoning-verification/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>ai-reasoning-verification</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/ai-reasoning-verification/</fr:route><fr:title text="AI Reasoning Verification: Stepwise Verification via NLI">AI Reasoning Verification: Stepwise Verification via NLI</fr:title><fr:meta name="institution">MPhil Thesis | <fr:link href="https://www.cam.ac.uk/" type="external">University of Cambridge</fr:link></fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisor: Prof. Andreas Vlachos</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Formulated NLI-based reformulations for stepwise verification, demonstrating that discriminative SFT significantly outperforms generative training (Supported by the Alan Turing Institute).</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  

  
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/l101-sft-rl-mechanisms/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>l101-sft-rl-mechanisms</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/l101-sft-rl-mechanisms/</fr:route><fr:title text="Representation vs. Execution: SFT and RL under Distribution Shift">Representation vs. Execution: SFT and RL under Distribution Shift</fr:title><fr:meta name="institution">Cambridge MPhil Project (L101)</fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisors: Prof. Andreas Vlachos &amp; Dr. Pushkar Mishra</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Investigated post-training dynamics (SFT vs. GRPO) under distributional shifts on Qwen2.5-1.5B using Structural Causal Models and layer-wise linear probing. Demonstrated that outcome-based RL restores syntactic compliance (98.2%) while latent causal rules remain internally encoded (66% probe accuracy) despite generative execution failure.</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  

  
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/l193-sparse-feature-circuits/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>l193-sparse-feature-circuits</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/l193-sparse-feature-circuits/</fr:route><fr:title text="Sparse Feature Circuits in Sycophantic Behaviour">Sparse Feature Circuits in Sycophantic Behaviour</fr:title><fr:meta name="institution">Cambridge MPhil Project (L193)</fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisors: Prof. Mateja Jamnik, Mateo Espinosa Zarlenga &amp; Dr. Zohreh Shams</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Applied <fr:link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19647" type="external">Sparse Feature Circuits (SFC) and SHIFT framework</fr:link> with JumpReLU SAEs (Gemma Scope) on Gemma-2-2B to analyze model sycophancy across NLP, Philosophy, and Political domains. Showed that targeted ablation of $10^2 - 10^3$ sparse features reduces sycophancy rates from 85.2% to 45.2% with minimal capability degradation.</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  

  
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2025</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/marl-social-dilemmas/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>marl-social-dilemmas</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/marl-social-dilemmas/</fr:route><fr:title text="Multi-Agent RL: Algorithm Design for Social Dilemmas">Multi-Agent RL: Algorithm Design for Social Dilemmas</fr:title><fr:meta name="institution">BSc Thesis | <fr:link href="https://www.kcl.ac.uk/" type="external">King's College London</fr:link></fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisor: Dr. Milad Kazemi</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Designed and reproduced multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) algorithms to resolve equilibrium convergence in non-zero-sum social dilemmas by extending XuanCe.</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  
